feat(tools): web fetch — the first real agent tool (fetch_url)

Turns a scheduled "briefing" agent from "recite what it knows" into "go
read the web." Also the template for every tool that follows.

JAIL MODEL: the daemon has NO network (Seatbelt). So fetch_url routes
through the privileged Rust side — same mediated-reach model as the path
broker, applied to the internet. The jailed brain never opens a socket;
it gets back only extracted text.

- web.rs: async reqwest GET (redirects, 20s timeout, 5MB body cap, 40k
  char cap), http(s)-only, basic SSRF guard (blocks localhost / private
  ranges / cloud metadata / .local). Dependency-free HTML->text: drops
  script/style/head/svg blocks, block-tags->newlines, strips tags,
  decodes common entities, collapses whitespace. Non-HTML (json/text/md)
  passes through. fetch_blocking bridges the async fetch to the sync
  tool-exec path (same pattern as the embed bridge).
- lib.rs: exec_tool_cfg handles fetch_url; builtin_tool_schema advertises
  it; AGENT_SYSTEM prompt tells the agent it has fetch_url for current info.
- tools_registry: register builtin.web / "Web Fetch" so it shows in the
  Tools tab and can be enabled per agent (like the PDF tool).

First compile of web.rs + fetch_blocking thread::scope bridge — flag if
cargo throws (the runtime-handle bridge is the riskiest bit).
